meta {
  name: Connect
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseURL}}/google-calendar/connect/
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
}

docs {
  Returns Google's consent-screen URL as JSON ({"data": {"auth_url": "..."}}).
  The frontend navigates the browser there itself.

  No body needed — this doesn't name which associate is connecting; identity
  is worked out later, after consent, from the connected Google account's own
  email (matched against User.email for an active Admin or Associate).
  Whichever Google account the staff member actually signs into on the
  consent screen decides who gets connected — not whoever called /connect/.

  The auth_url carries a signed state=<blob> (google_oauth.sign_oauth_state,
  flow="shared") — Google echoes it back verbatim to /callback/, which is how
  that view tells this flow apart from PersonalConnect.bru's, and (being
  signed) rejects it outright if it's tampered with in transit. Not a CSRF
  nonce; see docs/google-calendar-integration.md for why one isn't needed.

  Method: POST
  Path: /google-calendar/connect/
  Source: integrations.google_calendar.views.GoogleCalendarConnectView
  Auth: None — authentication_classes = [] (this view never reads request.user; identity is resolved after consent instead, see above).

  Manual end-to-end flow (no real frontend running locally):
  1. Run this request. Copy data.auth_url from the response.
  2. Paste auth_url into a real browser, signed in as the Google account
     you want to connect. Approve consent.
  3. Google redirects your browser to DEV_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI - our own
     /callback/ endpoint (see Callback.bru) - which now exchanges the code
     and saves the tokens itself, then redirects again to
     ADMIN_FRONTEND_URL/#gcal_connected=1 (or #gcal_error=<message> on
     failure). No separate request to run and no ticket to copy-paste - the
     whole thing completes from one browser round trip.
  4. Confirm it worked with Status.bru, or by checking
     GoogleCalendarConnection in the Django shell.
}
